xnu: a graphics boot replays the early log to the UART

vcattach() replays the message buffer (everything printed before the console came up)
through vcputc() when the boot is a text boot; SiliconForest's vcputc() mirrors each
character to the UART, and that replay is the only way those lines reach a serial
console. A graphics boot (Video.v_display 1) skips the replay, so its serial log lacked
them: boot qb01 on the M1 (graphics) against sm35 (text), same collection and boot-args,
had none of the 73 early lines ("kprintf initialized", the interrupt controller mapping,
the scheduler and IPC subsystem lines, ...). With serial output configured, a graphics
boot now sends the same bytes to the UART and still paints nothing.
UPSTREAM: yes.

--- a/src/Kernel/xnu/osfmk/console/video_console.c
+++ b/src/Kernel/xnu/osfmk/console/video_console.c
@@ -3159,6 +3159,26 @@
 				vcputc( '\r' );
 			}
 		}
+	} else if (serialmode & SERIALMODE_OUTPUT) {
+		long index;
+
+		// PD: a graphics boot paints no text, so the replay above (which vcputc also
+		// mirrors to the UART) does not run; the UART still gets the log from before
+		// the console came up, byte for byte as the text boot sends it
+		for (index = 0; index < msgbufp->msg_bufx; index++) {
+			char c = msgbufp->msg_bufc[index];
+
+			if (c == '\0') {
+				continue;
+			}
+			if (c == '\n') {
+				serial_putc('\r');
+			}
+			serial_putc(c);
+			if (c == '\n') {
+				serial_putc('\r');
+			}
+		}
 	}
 }
 
